NAME
  jobs, fg, bg - shell job control

SYNOPSIS
  jobs
  fg [%JOB]
  bg [%JOB]
  wait [%JOB]

DESCRIPTION
  A command started with a trailing '&' runs in the background and becomes a
  numbered job. Every stage of a background pipeline shares one process group,
  so a job is signalled, suspended, or resumed as a unit.

  jobs lists the jobs the shell still tracks with their state (running or
  stopped) and the pids that are still alive. Finished jobs are reported once
  at the next prompt and then dropped. The table is bounded; when it is full of
  running jobs a new background job is refused instead of losing track of a
  live one.

  fg resumes a stopped job in the foreground and waits for it. The job owns the
  terminal while it runs, so Ctrl+C interrupts it and Ctrl+Z suspends it again.

  bg resumes a stopped job in the background.

  wait blocks until the selected job terminates, or until every tracked job has
  terminated when no argument is given. A suspended job reports status 148
  (128 + SIGTSTP) instead of blocking forever.

  %JOB is one of:
    %n       job number
    %+       most recent job
    %-       the job before %+
    %prefix  first job whose command text starts with prefix

  Only the shell can see its own jobs. A job started by a script is tracked by
  that script's shell, so `wait` works there but prints no job notice.

EXAMPLES
  sleep 300 &
    Start a background job and print its number.

  jobs
    List background jobs.

  fg %1
    Continue job 1 in the foreground.

  bg %1
    Continue job 1 in the background.

  wait %1
    Block until job 1 exits.

SEE ALSO
  sh, kill, ps
